\chapter{Peer reviews}
\label{Reviews}

In FBF's peer-feedback activities students submit their work, review the
work of a few peers, rate the reviews they got, and reflect on the whole.
Canvas sees none of it, only the grade that FBF computes in the end.
The [[reviews]] command lists what the students wrote: by default the
reviews, one row per reviewer, reviewee and criterion; with [[--feedback]]
the reviewees' ratings of the reviews they got; with [[--reflections]] the
reflections.

\section{How FBF stores a peer review}

We learned the structure by following the API's relationships from a
peer-feedback activity; \cref{review-model} shows what we found.
\begin{description}
\item[Activity and tasks.] A peer-feedback activity has six tasks, one per
  step of the students' work: instructions, submission, review, viewing the
  reviews received, reflection and a grade.
  Three of them matter here.
  The [[submission-review-tasks]] resource has the [[criteria]] and the
  [[allocations]].
  The [[submission-review-view-tasks]] resource says whether the
  reviewees may rate the reviews they got
  ([[reviewer-feedback-enabled]]).
  The [[reflect-tasks]] resource has the [[reflections]].
\item[Allocations.] An allocation ([[assignments/allocation]]) pairs one
  reviewer, its [[actor]], with one reviewee, its [[allocable]].
  Both are users in every activity we have seen; the task's
  [[actor-type]] and [[allocable-type]] suggest that groups are possible
  too.
  Everything the reviewer wrote hangs off the allocation, as [[reviews]] and
  [[ratings]], and the reviewee's opinion of it as [[reviewer-feedback]].
  The attributes [[reviews-count]] and [[ratings-count]] say how many there
  are, which saves a request per empty list.
\item[Criteria.] A criterion is either a comment criterion
  ([[assignments/criterion]]), which asks for text only, or a rubric
  criterion ([[assignments/rubric-criterion]]), whose [[options]] are the
  levels to choose from ([[assignments/criterion-option]], each with a
  [[title]] and a [[weight]]).
  Criteria have an [[order]], which is the order of the review form.
\item[Reviews and ratings.] What FBF calls a review ([[reviews]]) is a
  comment on one criterion; there may be several per criterion, and rubric
  criteria get comments too.
  Its [[parent]] tells whether it is about the work as a whole (the
  activity) or about a submitted file ([[assignments/submission]]).
  A rating ([[ratings]]) is the level chosen on a rubric criterion, the
  [[option]]; it has no value of its own, so a score is the option's
  [[weight]].
\item[Feedback and reflections.] A reviewer feedback
  ([[reviewer-feedbacks]]) has a [[rating]], a number, and a [[body]].
  A reflection ([[reflections]]) has a [[user]] and a [[body]].
\end{description}

\begin{figure}
\centering
\begin{verbatim}
activity --tasks--> submission-review-tasks,
                    submission-review-view-tasks,
                    reflect-tasks, ...
submission-review-tasks --criteria--> (rubric-)criterion
(rubric-)criterion --options--> criterion-option
submission-review-tasks --allocations--> allocation
allocation: actor = reviewer, allocable = reviewee
allocation --reviews--> reviews: criterion,
                        parent = activity | submission
allocation --ratings--> ratings: criterion, option
allocation --reviewer-feedback--> reviewer-feedbacks
reflect-tasks --reflections--> reflections: user
\end{verbatim}
\caption{The resources of a peer review and their relationships.}
\label{review-model}
\end{figure}

