@

Several quirks of this part of the API shaped the code.
\begin{description}
\item[No includes.] FBF silently ignores [[include=]] on all the
  [[v1/assignments/...]] lists, and the relationships between the review
  resources are bare references without links.
  So we fetch each kind of resource as a list of its own and join them by
  id ourselves, as the discussions do with annotations
  (\cref{Discussions}).
\item[No bulk lists.] There is no list of all reviews of a task.
  The reviews and ratings come one list per allocation, some 800 requests
  for an activity of 270 allocations; we send them in parallel with
  [[get_many]] (\cref{Parallel}), and only for allocations whose counts are
  not zero.
  Two endpoints look like shortcuts but must not be used:
  [[v1/assignments/reviews]] with a filter never answers, and
  [[v1/assignments/ratings]] ignores its filters and returns ratings from
  other courses too.
  Nor do the reviews of a submission do: they miss the reviews of the work
  as a whole, whose parent is the activity.
\item[Levels by id.] The options of the rubric criteria are fetched by id
  in one request,
  \begin{center}
  [[v1/assignments/criterion_options?filter[id]=...]],
  \end{center}
  since a filter by criterion is refused.
\item[Rich text.] Reviews and reviewer feedback have plain text bodies, but
  a reflection's body is a Quill Delta, the document format of the editor
  that the students write it in.
\end{description}

The module separates fetching from assembling, as the discussions do.
Fetching follows the relationships above and returns resources; assembling
joins them into reviews without touching the network, which is where the
decisions are and where tests reach them with resources shaped like FBF's.

