\section{What we do not know}

The activities we explored had users as reviewers and reviewees, and
comment and rubric criteria only.
FBF has more: the task's [[actor-type]] and [[allocable-type]] suggest
group reviews, and the editor offers scale criteria.
We do not know how their data look, and guessing could print wrong rows
that look right: a group's id taken for a user's, a scale value taken for a
level.
So the code checks the types it relies on and raises
[[UnknownTypeError]], naming the type, for anything else.
It is a [[LookupError]], so the command line reports its message rather
than a traceback (\cref{CLI}).

<<classes>>=
class UnknownTypeError(LookupError):
    """FBF data of a type that fbf does not know how to read."""
<<constants>>=
CRITERION_TYPES = ["assignments/criterion", "assignments/rubric-criterion"]
PERSON_TYPE = "users"
@

A reviewer or reviewee is the related id of the allocation's [[actor]] or
[[allocable]], once we know that it is a user.

<<functions>>=
def person(allocation, name):
    """Return the user id of the `actor` or `allocable` of `allocation`."""
    data = relationship(allocation, name).get("data") or {}
    if data.get("type") != PERSON_TYPE:
        raise UnknownTypeError(
            f"allocation {name} of type {data.get('type')!r}: only "
            "reviews between users are supported")
    return data["id"]


def check_criteria(criteria):
    """Raise `UnknownTypeError` for criteria of a type we cannot read."""
    for criterion in criteria:
        if criterion["type"] not in CRITERION_TYPES:
            raise UnknownTypeError(
                f"criterion of type {criterion['type']!r}: only comment "
                "and rubric criteria are supported")
<<imports>>=
from fbf.schema import attribute, related_id, relationship, requires
