\section{The text of a body}

Reviews and reviewer feedback have bodies that are strings.
A reflection's body is a Quill Delta: a dictionary whose [[ops]] list the
pieces of the text in order.
A piece's [[insert]] is either a string, possibly with formatting
[[attributes]] that we drop, or an embedded object.
The only embed we have seen is an emoji, whose [[unicode]] is the code
point in hexadecimal, or several joined by hyphens for composed emoji; we
turn it into the character.
Other embeds, an image say, we show as their name in brackets, so that the
reader sees that something was there.
We read the Delta rather than FBF's rendering of it ([[body-rendered]]),
which is HTML and would need its tags removed.
A body we cannot read is an error, for the reasons above.

<<functions>>=
def plain_text(body):
    """Return the text of a `body`: a string or a Quill Delta."""
    if body is None:
        return ""
    if isinstance(body, str):
        return body
    if isinstance(body, dict) and isinstance(body.get("ops"), list):
        return "".join(insert_text(op.get("insert")) for op in body["ops"])
    raise UnknownTypeError(f"body of unknown format {type(body).__name__}")


def insert_text(insert):
    """Return the text of one Quill Delta `insert`."""
    if isinstance(insert, str):
        return insert
    if not isinstance(insert, dict):
        return ""
    if "emoji" in insert:
        return emoji(insert["emoji"])
    return "".join(f"[{name}]" for name in insert)


def emoji(embed):
    """Return the character of a Quill emoji `embed`, or `:name:`."""
    if not isinstance(embed, dict):
        return f":{embed}:" if isinstance(embed, str) and embed else ""
    try:
        return "".join(chr(int(code, 16))
                       for code in embed["unicode"].split("-"))
    except (KeyError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return f":{embed.get('name', '')}:"
@

Other Quill setups give an emoji as its name only, a string, so we show
that name between colons as well; and a code beyond Unicode's range is
handled like one that is not a number.
Either way a strange emoji costs a character, not the listing.

<<test functions>>=
def test_plain_text_of_strings_and_deltas():
  assert plain_text("Good work") == "Good work"
  assert plain_text(None) == ""
  delta = {"ops": [{"insert": "Bold", "attributes": {"bold": True}},
                   {"insert": " move "},
                   {"insert": {"emoji": {"name": "thumbsup",
                                         "unicode": "1f44d"}}},
                   {"insert": {"image": "https://example.com/x.png"}},
                   {"insert": "\n"}]}
  assert plain_text(delta) == "Bold move \U0001F44D[image]\n"
  with pytest.raises(UnknownTypeError):
    plain_text({"html": "<p>x</p>"})

def test_emoji_composed_and_unreadable():
  assert emoji({"unicode": "1f44d-1f3fb"}) == "\U0001F44D\U0001F3FB"
  assert emoji({"name": "smile", "unicode": "?"}) == ":smile:"
  assert emoji({"name": "big", "unicode": "f" * 40}) == ":big:"
  assert insert_text({"emoji": "smile"}) == ":smile:"
  assert insert_text({"emoji": None}) == ""
@


\section{Assembling the reviews}

The reviews of an activity are assembled from five lists: the allocations,
the reviews and ratings of all allocations, the criteria, and the options of
the rubric criteria, plus the users.
Reviews and ratings point to their allocation and criterion, so we group
them by that pair, and each pair that has a rating or a comment becomes a
[[Review]].
Pairs with neither, the criteria a reviewer skipped or the allocations of a
reviewer who never started, give no row: the output lists what was written,
and an empty row per skipped criterion would drown it.

We take the reviewer and reviewee from the allocation, not from the
review's own [[user]]: a rating or a comment always belongs to an
allocation, and the allocation names both parties.

FBF has stored at most one rating per allocation and criterion in the
activities we explored, as the review form allows one level per criterion.
Should there be more, we keep the latest, which is the level the reviewer
ended up with.

<<functions>>=
def make_reviews(activity, allocations, reviews, ratings, criteria,
                 options, users):
    """Return the `Review`s of an activity, assembled from its resources.

    `reviews` and `ratings` are those of all `allocations`; `options` maps
    option ids to the options of the rubric `criteria`, `users` user ids
    to users.  The result is sorted by reviewee, reviewer and criterion.
    """
    check_criteria(criteria)
    <<group comments and ratings by allocation and criterion>>
    result, placed = [], set()
    for allocation in allocations:
        reviewer = name_of(users, person(allocation, "actor"))
        reviewee = name_of(users, person(allocation, "allocable"))
        for criterion in sorted(criteria,
                                key=lambda c: attribute(c, "order")):
            key = (allocation["id"], criterion["id"])
            if key in comments or key in chosen:
                placed.add(key)
                <<add the [[Review]] of [[key]] to [[result]]>>
    <<refuse comments and ratings that were not placed>>
    return sorted(result, key=lambda review: (review.reviewee,
                                              review.reviewer))
@

Sorting the criteria by their order first and then the reviews by the people
keeps the criteria in form order within each reviewer and reviewee, since
Python's sort is stable.

A comment's [[on]] says whether it is about the work or about a file;
a parent of another type is shown by its type, so that it shows up rather
than passing for one of the two.
Comments are listed in the order they were written, and the latest rating
wins, as discussed above.

<<constants>>=
PARENT_KINDS = {"activities": "activity",
                "assignments/submission": "submission"}
<<group comments and ratings by allocation and criterion>>=
comments, chosen = {}, {}
for review in sorted(reviews, key=lambda r: attribute(r, "created-at")):
    key = (related_id(review, "allocation"), related_id(review, "criterion"))
    comments.setdefault(key, []).append(make_comment(review))
for rating in sorted(ratings, key=lambda r: attribute(r, "created-at")):
    key = (related_id(rating, "allocation"), related_id(rating, "criterion"))
    chosen[key] = related_id(rating, "option")
@

<<functions>>=
def make_comment(review):
    """Return the `Comment` of a review resource."""
    parent = relationship(review, "parent").get("data") or {}
    return Comment(on=PARENT_KINDS.get(parent.get("type"),
                                       parent.get("type") or ""),
                   created=attribute(review, "created-at"),
                   body=plain_text(attribute(review, "body")))
@

The level and score come from the chosen option.
The options are fetched by id, and an option that did not come back would
leave the level empty; that would be a wrong row that looks right, so it
is an error instead.

<<add the [[Review]] of [[key]] to [[result]]>>=
level, score = "", None
if key in chosen:
    option = options.get(chosen[key])
    if option is None:
        raise LookupError(f"criterion option {chosen[key]} not found")
    level, score = attribute(option, "title"), attribute(option, "weight")
result.append(Review(activity, reviewee, reviewer,
                     attribute(criterion, "title"),
                     level, score, comments.get(key, [])))
@

Every comment and rating should land in some [[Review]].
One whose allocation or criterion is not among those we were given would
otherwise be dropped silently, and the listing would look complete while it
is not; so we keep track of the pairs we placed and refuse to go on if any
are left.
The message gives the count and the ids, never the texts.

<<refuse comments and ratings that were not placed>>=
for name, found in [("comments", comments), ("ratings", chosen)]:
    left = sorted(set(found) - placed)
    if left:
        raise LookupError(
            f"{name} on {len(left)} unknown (allocation, criterion) "
            f"pairs: {left[:UNPLACED_SHOWN]}")
<<constants>>=
UNPLACED_SHOWN = 5
@

A user who has left the course may be missing from the users we get, and
then the name is empty rather than the whole listing failing.

<<functions>>=
def name_of(users, id):
    """Return the name of the user `id` in `users`, or an empty string."""
    user = users.get(id)
    return attribute(user, "name") if user else ""
@

Let's verify the assembly on a small activity: one rubric and one comment
criterion, two allocations, and a third allocation without anything.

<<test functions>>=
def criterion(id, title, order, options=(), rubric=True):
  kind = "assignments/rubric-criterion" if rubric else "assignments/criterion"
  return {"type": kind, "id": id,
          "attributes": {"title": title, "order": order},
          "relationships": {"options": {"data": [
            {"type": "assignments/criterion-option", "id": o}
            for o in options]}}}

def option(id, title, weight):
  return {"type": "assignments/criterion-option", "id": id,
          "attributes": {"title": title, "weight": weight}}

def review(id, alloc, crit, body, created, parent="activities"):
  return {"type": "reviews", "id": id,
          "attributes": {"body": body, "created-at": created},
          "relationships": {"allocation": ref("assignments/allocation", alloc),
                            "criterion": ref("x", crit),
                            "parent": ref(parent, "p")}}

def rating(id, alloc, crit, opt, created="2026-10-01T10:00Z"):
  return {"type": "ratings", "id": id,
          "attributes": {"created-at": created},
          "relationships": {"allocation": ref("assignments/allocation", alloc),
                            "criterion": ref("x", crit),
                            "option": ref("assignments/criterion-option",
                                          opt)}}

def user(id, name):
  return {"type": "users", "id": id, "attributes": {"name": name}}

CRITERIA = [criterion("c2", "Overall", 1, rubric=False),
            criterion("c1", "Clarity", 0, options=["o0", "o1"])]
OPTIONS = {"o0": option("o0", "Unclear", 0), "o1": option("o1", "Clear", 1)}
USERS = {"u1": user("u1", "Bo"), "u2": user("u2", "Ada"),
         "u3": user("u3", "Cy")}
@

Bo reviews Ada with a level and a comment on clarity and two comments
overall; Cy reviews Ada with a level only; Ada reviews Cy but never started.
The rows come sorted by reviewee, then reviewer, then criterion order,
whatever the order of the input; the comments come in the order written.

