\section{Fetching an activity's reviews}

An activity's tasks are bare references, so we ask for them with
[[include=tasks]] (see [[related]] in \cref{API}) and index them by type,
which is unique among an activity's tasks.
An activity without a review task is not a peer review; the command skips
it.

<<constants>>=
REVIEW_TASK = "submission-review-tasks"
VIEW_TASK = "submission-review-view-tasks"
REFLECT_TASK = "reflect-tasks"
<<functions>>=
def peer_review_tasks(fbf, activity_id):
    """Map task types to the tasks of a peer-review activity, or `None`."""
    document = fbf.get(f"v1/activities/{activity_id}", include="tasks")
    tasks = {kind: task for (kind, _), task in document.included.items()}
    return tasks if REVIEW_TASK in tasks else None
@

The criteria and the allocations are lists behind links of the review task.
The lists behind the allocations take one request each, which we send in
parallel and only for allocations whose count says the list is not empty.
[[allocation_lists]] does that for several relationships at once, so that
the reviews and the ratings share one pool of requests rather than waiting
for each other.
The [[wanted]] map gives, for each relationship, the test of whether an
allocation has anything there.

<<functions>>=
def allocation_lists(fbf, allocations, wanted):
    """Return the resources of the relationships `wanted` of `allocations`.

    `wanted` maps relationship names to a test of whether an allocation has
    any; allocations without are not asked.
    """
    asked = [(name, related_link(allocation, name))
             for allocation in allocations
             for name, has_any in wanted.items() if has_any(allocation)]
    documents = fbf.get_many([(link, {}) for _, link in asked])
    lists = {name: [] for name in wanted}
    for (name, _), document in zip(asked, documents):
        lists[name] += document.resources
    return lists


def counted(name):
    """Return a test of whether the count `name` of an allocation is > 0."""
    return lambda allocation: bool(attribute(allocation, name))
<<imports>>=
from fbf.discussions import related_link
@

The options are fetched by id once the ratings are in, from the ids that
the criteria list and that the ratings chose: a rating's option should be
among its criterion's, but asking for both costs nothing and makes the
error above impossible for an option that exists.
The users are fetched by id too, the reviewers and reviewees of all
allocations.
We check the criteria and the people before the hundreds of requests for
the lists, so that an activity we cannot read fails at once.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_reviews(fbf, tasks, activity):
    """Return the `Review`s of a peer-review activity with `tasks`.

    `activity` labels the reviews.
    """
    task = tasks[REVIEW_TASK]
    criteria = fbf.get(related_link(task, "criteria")).resources
    check_criteria(criteria)
    allocations = fbf.get(related_link(task, "allocations")).resources
    users = allocation_users(fbf, allocations)
    lists = allocation_lists(fbf, allocations,
                             {"reviews": counted("reviews-count"),
                              "ratings": counted("ratings-count")})
    option_ids = [r["id"] for criterion in criteria
                  for r in relationship(criterion, "options").get("data")
                  or []]
    option_ids += [related_id(r, "option") for r in lists["ratings"]]
    options = fbf.get_by_ids("v1/assignments/criterion_options", option_ids)
    return make_reviews(activity, allocations, lists["reviews"],
                        lists["ratings"], criteria, options, users)


def allocation_users(fbf, allocations):
    """Map the ids of the reviewers and reviewees to user resources."""
    return fbf.get_by_ids("v1/users",
                          [person(allocation, name)
                           for allocation in allocations
                           for name in ("actor", "allocable")])
@

These functions talk to FBF, so their test needs a recording
(\cref{Testing}); offline, the fake of \cref{fake-fbf} stands in.
The recorded test checks what holds for any peer review on any day: every
comment and every rating that the allocations count is in the result
exactly once, a review has a level exactly when it has a score, and every
level and score is the title and weight of one of the criteria's options.
That fails if we skip allocations we should not, lose comments in the
grouping, or read the levels from the wrong place.
The last check is stricter than [[fetch_reviews]]: a rating whose option
the rubric no longer lists would fail it.
We have never seen one in FBF (all 540 ratings we checked chose a listed
option), so a failure there means that this case has appeared, and the
check should then learn to accept it.
To keep the recording small it takes the smallest peer review in the
launched course, counted in allocations, among those that have ratings,
since one without would leave the levels unchecked.
The recorded tests of the feedback and the reflections below choose their
activity the same way.

<<test functions>>=
import conftest

def smallest_peer_review(fbf, wanted):
  """Return `(tasks, allocations)` of the smallest `wanted` peer review."""
  found = []
  for tasks in conftest.review_task_sets(fbf, fbf.login):
    allocations = fbf.get(related_link(tasks[REVIEW_TASK],
                                       "allocations")).resources
    if wanted(tasks, allocations):
      found.append((tasks, allocations))
  assert found, "no such peer review in the launched course"
  return min(found, key=lambda pair: len(pair[1]))

@pytest.mark.vcr
def test_fetch_reviews_accounts_for_every_comment_and_rating(fbf):
  tasks, allocations = smallest_peer_review(
    fbf, lambda tasks, allocations:
      any(a["attributes"]["ratings-count"] for a in allocations))
  result = fetch_reviews(fbf, tasks, "Activity")
  assert sum(len(r.comments) for r in result) \
    == sum(a["attributes"]["reviews-count"] for a in allocations)
  assert sum(r.level != "" for r in result) \
    == sum(a["attributes"]["ratings-count"] for a in allocations)
  assert all((r.level == "") == (r.score is None) for r in result)
  criteria = fbf.get(related_link(tasks[REVIEW_TASK], "criteria")).resources
  options = fbf.get_by_ids(
    "v1/assignments/criterion_options",
    [o["id"] for c in criteria
     for o in c["relationships"]["options"]["data"] or []])
  levels = {(o["attributes"]["title"], o["attributes"]["weight"])
            for o in options.values()}
  assert all((r.level, r.score) in levels for r in result if r.level)
