\section{The command}

The command works like [[discussions]] (\cref{Discussions}): it takes the
[[canvaslms]] assignment options, finds the FBF activities of the selected
assignments in their course round (\cref{Canvas}), and lists what was
written in each.
Assignments that are not FBF's are dropped, so [[-c]] alone lists every
peer review in a course.
Everything is labelled with the Canvas assignment name, by which the user
selected it, and comes in the order of the assignments.
The command itself is only the glue to [[canvaslms]]; the decisions are in
the functions it calls, where the tests can reach them.

<<imports>>=
import json
import sys
import canvaslms.cli
import canvaslms.cli.assignments
import canvaslms.cli.cache
import canvaslms.cli.login
from fbf import canvas as fbfcanvas
<<functions>>=
def reviews_command(config, canvas, args):
    """List the peer reviews of the selected FBF assignments."""
    assignments = [a for a in
                   canvaslms.cli.assignments.process_assignment_option(
                       canvas, args)
                   if fbfcanvas.placement_code(a)]
    if not assignments:
        raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError("No FeedbackFruits assignments")
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    items = []
    for assignment, fbf, activity in fbfcanvas.fbf_activities(
            canvaslms.cli.cache.normalize_hostname(hostname), token,
            assignments):
        try:
            items += activity_items(fbf, activity, assignment.name, args)
        except Skipped as reason:
            print(f"{assignment.name}: {reason}, skipped", file=sys.stderr)
    <<output [[items]] as [[args]] say>>
@

\subsection{What an activity gives}

Three kinds of activity give nothing, and the user should learn why rather
than see them missing: FBF activities that are not peer reviews, those not
yet opened this round, and, with [[--feedback]], peer reviews without
reviewer feedback.
[[activity_items]] raises [[Skipped]] with the reason for them, which the
command prints on standard error; it is not an error, so the other
activities are still listed.
The three views share everything else and differ in what they fetch, so
[[fetcher]] picks the fetch function by the options.

<<classes>>=
class Skipped(Exception):
    """An activity that gives nothing in the selected view, and why."""
<<functions>>=
def activity_items(fbf, activity, name, args):
    """Return the items of `activity` in the view that `args` select.

    `name` labels the items.  Raises `Skipped` with the reason when the
    activity has nothing to give.
    """
    if not activity:
        raise Skipped("not opened in FeedbackFruits this course round")
    tasks = peer_review_tasks(fbf, activity)
    if not tasks:
        raise Skipped("not a peer review")
    if args.feedback and not feedback_enabled(tasks):
        raise Skipped("reviewer feedback is not enabled")
    return fetcher(args)(fbf, tasks, name)


def fetcher(args):
    """Return the fetch function of the view that `args` select."""
    return (fetch_feedback if args.feedback
            else fetch_reflections if args.reflections
            else fetch_reviews)
@

The fake course of \cref{fake-fbf} serves all of these cases.
A namespace with the options stands in for the parsed command line, and
the skip for disabled feedback must not cost more than the request for
the tasks.

<<test functions>>=
import argparse

def view(**options):
  return argparse.Namespace(**{"feedback": False, "reflections": False,
                               "long": False, **options})

def test_activity_items_picks_the_view_and_says_why_it_skips():
  fbf = course_with_a_peer_review()
  for options, kind in [({}, Review), ({"feedback": True}, Feedback),
                        ({"reflections": True}, Reflection)]:
    items = activity_items(fbf, "act", "Essay", view(**options))
    assert [type(item) for item in items] == [kind, kind]
  with pytest.raises(Skipped, match="not opened"):
    activity_items(fbf, None, "Essay", view())
  with pytest.raises(Skipped, match="not a peer review"):
    activity_items(fbf, "video", "Essay", view())
  off = course_with_a_peer_review(feedback_on=False)
  with pytest.raises(Skipped, match="not enabled"):
    activity_items(off, "act", "Essay", view(feedback=True))
  assert off.asked == ["v1/activities/act"]
@

